  {
    "episode_id": 50982,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50982.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 11,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think there are a couple of reasons. One is I'm a good candidate and I'm going to be a good member of Congress. I have been a trial attorney for over 33 years for the last 25 years. I've been focusing almost exclusively on water, natural resources issues, water and natural resource issues, property rights and constitutional rights. And so I represent municipalities, counties, farmers, ranchers, resource producers, oil and gas companies and things like that. I have been fighting for Wyoming. And so I wasn't just running against Liz Cheney, which I think is what some people, especially back in DC, may think. I was running for Wyoming. I was running to address the issues that are important to us. So I traveled over 40,000 miles in Wyoming in the last 11 months. Prior to the primary, it was right at 40,000 miles. I've had over 200 public events. I hold two to three-hour town halls. I've gone out and met with the people and talked about the issues that were important to them. So from the standpoint of just comparing this, Liz Cheney lives in the McLean, Virginia area. Barry seldom came to Wyoming when she did would not have public events, never answered for her votes, never answered for the decisions that she made, and simply abandoned Wyoming. And I think what it exposed was she had used Wyoming for the last six years as a mechanism to get power, but she didn't actually represent Wyoming's interests. And I think that part of the reason it was such a blowout, almost 40 points, even beating the polls, was because people recognize that Harriet Hageman will represent them from Wyoming, and Liz Cheney has not.",
