  {
    "episode_id": 50982,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50982.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 12,
    "turn_text": "As I was watching the campaign, you know, you, like most candidates, did a big introductory ad at the beginning to say who you were. Then there were debates, then you did a big closing ad. And they focused just on Wyoming, Wyoming, Wyoming. land rights or water rights or energy or livestock or just ranching all sorts of Wyoming issues. And hers did not. A couple of data points when Dick Cheney, her father, the former vice president, made a two minute ad for her. And he spoke quite eloquently on behalf of his daughter. He never said the word Wyoming. And in her concession speech, election night, She did at the end say, you know, God bless Wyoming, God bless the United States. But before that, she said the word one time. And that was to say that there were election deniers in Wyoming as there are in other parts around the country. So I thought that was kind of striking because there's only one representative from Wyoming. There's not really a whole lot of people in Wyoming compared to some other states. And she never said the word.",
