    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50982.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 34,
    "turn_text": "So for many years now, I have been traveling the country giving a speech called Regulation Without Representation. And I have done a deep dive into the regulatory state and what it is in terms of being a hidden tax. Right now, the regulatory state, the hidden tax associated with that is about $2.1 trillion a year. That's your permitting. That's what you have to do in terms of trying to construct reservoirs. That is all of the requirements anymore on our trucking industry. All of those are attacks, but they're imposed by a regulatory agency. And that's the cost to what happens in this country just because of the regulatory state. In addition to which Congress has, especially the last 30 years, largely abdicated its responsibility of actually legislating. And they've turned much of the legislating over to regulatory agencies. EPA, US Fish and Wildlife Service, Forest Service, Department of Transportation, Department of Health and Human Services, NIH. You can go on at FBI, you can go on and on and on and on about all the three letter agencies. There's approximately 400 federal agencies. We don't even know how many, but what we've done is we are now being regulated and legislated by a bunch of unelected bureaucrats. And one of my primary focuses when I get into Congress is to write this ship, go back to the separation of powers, go back to our constitutional foundation where Congress is who writes the laws in the executive branch does nothing more than carry them out. So I've been involved with a nonprofit law firm for the last three years. And our sole purpose is addressing the unconstitutionality of the unlawful regulatory state. Now, are all regulations unlawful? No, I'm not saying that they are, but there are a lot of them that are. You know, an example was the USDA a couple of years ago issued a guidance document, a two-page guidance document saying that our livestock industry by 2023 has to use RFID a radio frequency identification ear tax. That would be a cost to our industry of over $2 billion that was just imposed by the USDA. All",
