    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50982.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 67,
    "turn_text": "Highways. One of the things that they're doing now that we're struggling with is Historically, truckers have always trained the next generation of truckers. So I have a nephew, for example, who owns two semis and he's a truck driver and then he trains other truckers and they work for him and he wants to build out a fleet and be a trucking company. So historically, that's how much of our training has been done. More recently, they are adopting regulations that all the training has to be done by these federal employees or contractors. Number one, they're not as good at training them as real truck drivers are. Number two, it's costing tens of thousands of dollars for each one of these. And what I always go back to with these kinds of things is, what is it you're trying to fix? Why is the agency all of a sudden deciding, okay, all of the truckers that have been as successful as they have been over the last 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70 years, all of a sudden you've got them coming in and saying, yeah, we're going to do that now. We're going to take that over. Well, again, it is to expand power of the agency. It's to increase resources and funds into the agency. And it is an illegal expansion of administrative oversight, trying to fix a problem that doesn't exist while at the same time really hurting the industry. The number of trucking companies and truck drivers who have come to me in the last six months and said, They're destroying our industry. They being the federal government. The Department of Transportation is destroying our industry. And they're making it impossible for us to get good truck drivers.",
