    "turn_text": "Well, one of the things I've done is I've already started talking to people about it. I started talking to some of the leadership in Congress. I've written about this extensively. I've handled numerous lawsuits on these exact issues. And obviously as one individual, I can't fix it. But what I can do is start building relationships and the allies and educating people. on the importance of this issue, because the reality is why we're in the mess that we're in, why we're in the mess with COVID, why we have almost $32 trillion in debt at this point. We have out of control agencies, as I've already talked about, in terms of just some of the things that they've done that are almost insignificant. The federal register's over 95,000 pages a year. the document or the book where all of these things are published. That gives you an idea of the magnitude of what we're talking about in terms of these regulations. So it's a part education and then it's a part working with my fellow congressional representatives so that they can understand the significance of this for the real people on the ground. Unlike someone like Liz Cheney, who's really only ever been Liz Cheney, I've been out there working. I've been working for the irrigation districts and trying to help them to protect their infrastructure and the things that they have to go through to rebuild infrastructure. I've been working for the municipalities attempting to build water treatment plants and what they have to go through with the EPA to have that done. I've worked for the gravel companies to pave highways And the regulations and the hundreds of thousands of dollars that they spend on permitting. So these are real life experiences that I've had that I think some of the people that we have in Congress back in Washington DC don't have and don't understand.",
