    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50982.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 73,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think there's two things that you need to consider when you're looking at what's going on between Russia and the Ukraine. First of all, how did we get to this point? And I think that it is imperative that we understand that the failed energy policy, not only of the Biden administration, but the Obama administration and the Clinton administration before that. And you'll notice that I'm skipping the people who are actually advocates for oil and gas development and use of our own and being energy independent and using our own resources. The Western world has been almost on a mutual suicide pact for the last 25 years. And that is Germany, much of Europe, the United States, Canada. We are attempting under a very misguided idea of, we're attempting to make it so that we don't produce our own energy. But we all know that we've got to find the energy somewhere, right? So what we've done is we've empowered the dictators and the despots at the expense of national security and the protection of our own individual citizens. So Russia has been empowered because Europe is dependent on Russia for oil and gas. It's right now they're talking in Germany and England. If you read any of the articles about what they're facing for this winter, it's catastrophic because they are so dependent upon a dictator in Russia, providing them with the oil and gas that they need to be able to run their economies and run their countries. So failed energy policy is the reason why or much of the reason why you've got the battle between the Ukraine and Russia right now anyway, or that empowered Russia to actually invade. I realize there's a long history there. But you've got a lot of countries in this world who are incapable of taking care of themselves.",
