    "turn_text": "Release 14,000 hours of videotape. Everything that happened that day should be out in the open. We ought to be able to see what happened. We need to find out to what extent the FBI or any other agency had people embedded. We need to understand to what extent Nancy Pelosi, Muriel Bowser, were offered or had available to them resources that they rejected. We need to understand what is behind and what we see on some of the videotape that has been released with officers opening doors and people wandering in and taking selfies. So when you say what should be done, I'm all for investigating it. I'm all for finding out what happened on January 6th. I'm all for finding out what the failures were. I'm all for finding out who all was involved, but not a one-sided Kafka-esque type of approach where, you know, I keep harkening back to the book by Franz Kafka, the trial. And I really do think that Liz Cheney and the other people on that committee ought to read it because it's almost an autobiography for them. It is just, it's stunning to me that we've got people who are attorneys and non-attorneys who do not believe in due process, do not believe in cross-examination, do not believe in confrontation. One of the funniest things I heard Liz Cheney say, and this really does demonstrate that she's not a trial attorney. Somebody made a comment about, well, don't you think that there should be cross-examination? And she said, cross-examination wouldn't change any of this. And I thought, oh my gosh, just let me a couple of them. I'll tell you what I can change. Just a, just a, even a little bit across examination, you'd have a completely different narrative coming out of that.",
