  {
    "episode_id": 50982,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50982.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 109,
    "turn_text": "that's where when Liz Cheney wants to say, well, there are election deniers or there are people who are going to always give an excuse to Donald Trump. That's not what we're doing. What we're saying is, Why have you treated these people so dramatically different than these people over here? Because one of the things that happened during our debate, by the way, we only had one debate because that's all that Liz Cheney would show up in Wyoming for, she flew in and flew out. But despite that, one of the things that was asked was, do you see Donald Trump as she said that Donald Trump is the biggest threat to our democracy in our history? And I said, first of all, Donald Trump is not a threat to our republic, we're not a democracy, we're a republic. But what is really a threat to our republic is two tiers, two different types of justice system, depending on who you are. If you're Hillary Clinton or Hunter Biden, you get treated one way. If you're somebody who is at the Capitol grounds on January 6, you get treated a different way. You want to see a country torn asunder. You treat people differently, depending on who they are and what their political connections are. That's a threat to our republic. If we have two different types of justice systems, depending on whether you're a liberal or you're a conservative, a Democrat or a Republican, that's a threat to our republic.",
