  {
    "episode_id": 50982,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50982.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 115,
    "turn_text": "I absolutely do, but one of the things that does concern me is, as an attorney, we will look at all different kinds of theories, and we'll do all different kinds of legal analyses, whether we're filing a complaint or we're defending against a complaint that has been filed. Some of them are viable and some of them are not. One of the things that I am very concerned about is the criminalization of legal analysis. And that's part of what I do think that it's been interesting, and I think it's been very helpful to pull the curtain back a little bit and see how some of these people think. I don't have a problem with people throwing ideas out on the table. talking about them, refuting them, saying no, saying yes, that's the way we always get to the right answer. When at some point you say, you can't say that, or you can't do that, or you can't believe that, and then you attempt to have the government come in and punish you for thinking a certain way, is to me extremely dangerous. So the idea, there were people that have absolute legitimate concerns about what happened in the 2020 election. What happened with Zuckerberg and his $500 million? What happened in Wisconsin? What happened in Pennsylvania? There are legitimate questions. Everybody has the right to question what our government has done. And when I see John Eastman or some of the other attorneys throw out ideas of what they could do, they were looking at what they thought was the effort to steal the election by the Biden administration. He's our president. That's life. We've largely gone on from that. But I don't like the idea that you can take an attorney and say, well, you came up with a memo or an analysis, and we disagree with it. And therefore, we're going to criminalize you. That isn't the way our legal system works.",
